    <h2 style="color:#0f1f3d;margin-top:40px;">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>How do I find the right DINWEYS battery for my truck model?</h3>
      <p>Find your truck model above. Each listed model shows the OEM battery group (e.g. N150/N200) and the corresponding DINWEYS model (145G51/190H52). These are industry references — always confirm your vehicle's group size, terminal orientation and tray dimensions before ordering.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>Are these fitment listings officially confirmed by the vehicle manufacturers?</h3>
      <p>No. The battery groups are sourced from independent commercial-vehicle fitment guides (Club Assist and Century Batteries). They are industry references, not DINWEYS official OEM confirmations or vehicle-manufacturer endorsements. Verify against your vehicle specification.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>What do the fitment confidence levels mean?</h3>
      <p>HIGH means multiple credible sources or manufacturer documentation; MEDIUM means a credible independent source with matching technical parameters; LOW means a limited third-party reference; UNVERIFIED means insufficient evidence. Every listing above shows its level, and none are OEM confirmations.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>Why do some models show "needs inquiry" instead of a DINWEYS model?</h3>
      <p>Because their OEM battery group (for example JIS N100L, N120 or the smaller 55D23L/75D23L classes) is not currently in the DINWEYS standard product line. Contact DINWEYS to confirm whether a compatible model or custom solution is available.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>Does a 24V truck need two batteries?</h3>
      <p>Yes. A 24V truck electrical system uses two 12V batteries wired in series. Always replace both together with identical models to keep charging balanced.</p>
    </div>

